  <section aria-labelledby="bio-title">
    <h2 id="bio-title">Biography</h2>
    <div class="bio-grid">
      <div class="bio-card">
        <h3>Survival as practice</h3>
        <p>Raised in East Texas and later serving in the U.S. Marine Corps, Erin became a long-time resident of the Eighth and Harrison encampment in Berkeley. The work there has included community cleanup, public comment, civil disobedience, legal challenges to encampment sweeps, and maintaining space for people arriving with nowhere else to settle.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bio-card">
        <h3>The Hovel</h3>
        <p>The Hovel was built and maintained at the encampment as practical shelter and community infrastructure. Public reporting documents it as a place intended to make room for people who arrive needing somewhere to be, rather than treating survival as an individual failure.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bio-card">
        <h3>Articles of Survival</h3>
        <p>The <em>Articles of Survival</em> begin with a compact operating constraint: “Do no harm.” They extend lived experience into a written framework for survival, relationship, responsibility, and mutual dependence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bio-card">
        <h3>The Interdependent Way</h3>
        <p>The Interdependency LLC turns the same premise into research and technical experiments: spend on compute and tools, preserve evidence, expose uncertainty, and publish what the work actually produces instead of substituting claims for results.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="fine">Public profile sources: <a href="https://thestreetspirit.org/2025/05/07/you-cant-say-we-didnt-clean-up/">Street Spirit on The Hovel</a> · <a href="https://thestreetspirit.org/2025/07/03/no-different-than-the-stars-in-the-sky-life-in-writing-with-erin-spencer/">Street Spirit interview and Articles of Survival</a>.</p>
  </section>

